{:edb/db-before-t 1259 :edb/db-after-t 1260 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534572 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:20:54.827Z" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068455 1145 "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} &= \\lim_{x\\to3} \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {3x^2}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3(3)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 false] [17592186068455 1145 "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n}\n$$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068456 1100 "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068456 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}$." 13194139534572 false] [17592186068456 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nso we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nBut that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ &=4. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 false] [17592186068456 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\n&=4.\n}\n$$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068457 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068457 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)}$." 13194139534572 false] [17592186068457 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty\n$$\n\nrespectively,\nso we have the case\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\n.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 0. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 false] [17592186068457 1145 "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068458 1100 "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068458 1100 "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4}}$." 13194139534572 false] [17592186068458 1145 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\nso applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {1}{5} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 false] [17592186068458 1145 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186068612 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296238 13194139534572 true] [17592186068612 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296245 13194139534572 true] [17592186068612 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296252 13194139534572 true] [17592186068612 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296263 13194139534572 true] [17592186068612 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296267 13194139534572 true] [17592186068613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296217 13194139534572 true] [17592186068613 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296270 13194139534572 true] [17592186068614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296224 13194139534572 true] [17592186068614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296231 13194139534572 true] [17592186068614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296256 13194139534572 true] [17592186068614 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296260 13194139534572 true] [17592186068615 1020 #edb/ref 17592186296274 13194139534572 true] [17592186069170 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186069170 1100 "What is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2\\ln x}{e^{x}}$?" 13194139534572 false] [17592186264237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296273 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186296218 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1089 "q-114252" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1113 #uuid "e4290f82-8e72-5fbe-9469-7463c9848c9b" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296217 1145 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x-2){e}^{-x}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0-2){e}^{0}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+4}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296219 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296220 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296221 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296222 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186296223 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1041 "selection" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1104 #edb/ref 17592186296223 13194139534572 true] [17592186296218 1110 #uuid "0ceb23dd-97df-5bf4-817d-999f2b55060d" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296219 1034 "3" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534572 true] [17592186296219 1111 #uuid "19380428-ddeb-5bea-a1fd-1060af3fee38" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296220 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534572 true] [17592186296220 1111 #uuid "2fc1ce76-ddf9-5c3c-997b-fe9820824bab" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296221 1034 "2" 13194139534572 true] [17592186296221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534572 true] [17592186296221 1111 